<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chitradurga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chitradurga is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for its hill fortress with seven concentric rings of walls on granite hills. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a> were found nearby. The fort was enlarged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> of Chitradurga, who ruled from it from the 16th century until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> took it in 1779. Local legend celebrates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onake-obavva">Onake Obavva</a>, who defended a breach in the walls with a pestle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chitradurga.jpg" alt="Chitradurga" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basavarajmin21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GALIMANTAPA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chitradurga lies in the valley of the Vedavati river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and is the headquarters of Chitradurga district. It is a place of bold rock hills and valleys with towering boulders, and the boulders on which much of the city stands belong to the oldest rock formation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its names in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> include Kallina Kote, the stone fortress, Ukkina Kote, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> fort, and Yelusuttina Kote, the fort of seven circles. In the British period its official name was Chittaldrug. By legend from the Mahabharatha, a man-eating rakshasa named Hidimba lived on the hill until Bhima killed him in a duel and married his sister Hidimbi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Timmanna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a>, a chieftain under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, was made governor of Chitradurga as a reward for military service, which began the rule of the Nayakas of Chitradurga. His son Obana Nayaka was known as Madakari Nayaka. Later rulers included Kasturi Rangappa, who consolidated the kingdom, and Bharamappa Nayaka, who came to the throne in 1689 after the Dalawayis overthrew Madakari Nayaka III. Several brief reigns followed. The last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madakari-nayaka-v">Madakari Nayaka V</a> (1758–1779), lost the fort to a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>, after which the city came under Hyder Ali and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and then, after Tipu's defeat by the British, under the royal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>.</p><p>The fort was built between the 10th and 18th centuries by successive dynasties, the Rashtrakutas, Kalyana Chalukyas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, Vijayanagara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a>. It has seven enclosing walls, 19 gateways, 38 posterns, a palace, a mosque added under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>, granaries, oil pits and many tanks that collected rainwater. In the upper part stand eighteen temples, of which the Hidambeshwara temple is the oldest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 1,25,170 people in Chitradurga. The city is administered by a municipal council, and wind farms with a total installed capacity of 49.7 MW stand on the surrounding hills. The Chandravalli cave complex lies between three hills, with stone steps leading to underground rooms, and the pillow lava near the village of Maradihalli is a National Geological Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Local legend celebrates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onake-obavva">Onake Obavva</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>'s soldiers were found entering the fort through an opening in the rocks, while the guard had gone home for lunch, she killed them one by one with an onake, a wooden club for pounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, as they emerged. The opening and the well she was going to, the Tanniru doni, survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chitradurga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The best-studied sequence is at Brahmagiri, a granite outcrop in <b>Chitradurga</b> district rising about 180 metres above the surrounding plains.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nayakas of <b>Chitradurga</b> were Beda chiefs who served the Vijayanagara Empire as local military governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/chitradurga-nayakas">Chitradurga Nayakas</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nolambas, who called themselves Nolamba Pallavas and claimed descent from the Pallava dynasty, emerged in the 8th century in Nolambavadi, a region covering parts of modern <b>Chitradurga</b>, Tumkur, Kolar and Anantapur.</blockquote><a href="/state/nolamba-dynasty">Nolamba dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
